WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.560
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:12.660
I was really looking forward to this paper. This&nbsp;
says that GPT-4, an AI has perhaps a spark of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:20.160
intelligence. That is a huge claim, and when we&nbsp;
looked into this paper earlier, we saw that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:27.780
can do incredible things. It finds its way around&nbsp;
in an imaginary maze, and even make a map for it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:35.280
And it also learned to see. Now why would that&nbsp;
be so important, there are tons of learning-based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:42.300
techniques out there that understand images&nbsp;
and even video. So why is this so crazy? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:51.420
it is not only crazy, but we thought it was flat&nbsp;
out impossible! Why? Well, because this AI never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:58.500
saw an image, just text and textual descriptions&nbsp;
of things, yet it can draw these. Outstanding.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:05.760
And its mathematical skills are nearly on the&nbsp;
level of the International Mathematical Olympiad.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:10.200
And today I can’t wait, because&nbsp;
we are going to have a deeper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:15.000
look together at what else it can&nbsp;
do, and things that it can’t. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.500
we will see good and bad and I&nbsp;
think you will be very surprised.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:28.380
First, when we ask how many prime numbers&nbsp;
are there between 150 and 250, it says 13.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:35.700
Without commenting on this, let’s rephrase the&nbsp;
question. Now, little AI, list the prime numbers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:44.520
between 150 and 250, then count them up.
Then, it starts listing, and…uh-oh.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:52.800
18. So, which one is it, 13 or 18?&nbsp;
Well, the correct answer is indeed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:59.700
18. This is insanity! The AI clearly has&nbsp;
the knowledge to solve this problem, yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:06.960
depending on how we ask, it either knows or it&nbsp;
doesn’t. So how is this even possible? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:13.140
the problem is that the first prompt is not&nbsp;
computerly enough. Let me explain - you see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:19.440
the second prompt allows it to think in a step&nbsp;
by step manner, which has been shown to lead to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:26.460
better results. Sometimes. And that is a huge&nbsp;
point. Why is that? Well, because of something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:34.440
that happened 73 years ago. Now I hear you asking,&nbsp;
what do you mean Károly? What is this insanity?

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:41.160
Well, Isaac Asimov, famous science-fiction&nbsp;
writer created an imaginary world with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:46.380
intelligent robots, that sometimes showed&nbsp;
unexpected behavior. In these cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:51.840
robopsychologists were called to find&nbsp;
out what is going on in the brain of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:58.740
this little AI. And he wrote this about 73&nbsp;
years ago. And today, here we are. The first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:05.760
robopsychologists are appearing in our real world.&nbsp;
How incredible is that? What a time to be alive!

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:11.580
Now, next one. Storytelling. Here,&nbsp;
the first letters are already given,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:20.100
it has to fill them so that it forms a story about&nbsp;
unicorns. And, we also ask that the story has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:27.900
twist to it. So, can it do it? Yes, it will do&nbsp;
it perfectly. No robopsychology is required here.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:32.340
But then, when we show it one of the&nbsp;
simplest math problem you can imagine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:39.180
and ask for the result of another one, it&nbsp;
fails. What in the world is happening? This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:45.900
AI can read and evaluate electrocardiograms as&nbsp;
well as some of the smartest humans out there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:50.700
but it often can’t solve a problem&nbsp;
that a child could easily solve.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:56.460
So this is the age we live in. We have an&nbsp;
AI that can create a nice little network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.460 --> 00:04:01.980
graph describing the relation of movie&nbsp;
titles, directors and writers with real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:08.340
data grabbed from IMDB, but keeps making&nbsp;
arithmetic errors on really simple tasks.

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:14.340
This is the age where an AI can write a&nbsp;
computer game in Javascript super quickly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:21.180
and solve classical fun thought experiments&nbsp;
with logic. It even uses common sense to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:27.360
out about the rules of the game by itself. And&nbsp;
it does it even better than its predecessor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:32.160
but it has no idea what year&nbsp;
it is. What is even worse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:36.660
it says different things at different&nbsp;
times, and all of them are wrong.

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:43.560
And I wonder what all of these will look like&nbsp;
just two more papers down the line. Now, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:49.740
video took a bit of time as I wanted the dust to&nbsp;
settle a bit and gather you the most interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:57.720
